stateDiagram-v2
  [*] --> authoring: gogo-plan creates .gogo/work/feature-slug/
  authoring --> authoring: analyst writes state.md / decisions.md / charts/
  authoring --> authoring: analyst dies mid-write (STAYS here - derived, crash-safe)
  authoring --> awaiting_acceptance: plan.md lands (present AND >= 2 '## ' sections)
  awaiting_acceptance --> plan_accepted: user accepts (in-chat, or m -> /gogo:accept)
  plan_accepted --> implementing: gogo-implement step 1 ENTRY write
  implementing --> reviewing: gogo-review entry write (or implement's step 4 EXIT write)
  reviewing --> testing: gogo-test entry write (or review's step 4 EXIT write)
  testing --> awaiting_uat: report 5
  implementing --> stalled: session killed, no live gogo-go
  stalled --> implementing: gogo go slug resumes (RunnableStatus is true)

  note right of authoring
    DERIVED, display-only. The status on disk
    is still awaiting-plan-acceptance (or
    absent); plan.md's absence or stub-ness is
    what makes it read as authoring. NO new
    status enum value, NO new class: it stays
    unfinished, in the plan column.
    Card: dim '✎ authoring', no gate stripe,
    footer chip '[m] ✗ plan not written'.
    m AND M refuse; gogo go, /gogo:accept and
    the unattended auto-pickup all refuse. Not
    counted in '⏸ K need you' nor in gogo
    status's WAIT column. The refusal names
    its number and its unblock.
  end note

  note right of plan_accepted
    BEFORE 0.29.0 the whole build ran INSIDE
    this state: gogo-implement wrote
    implementing only at its step 4, after the
    work. The card sat in the plan column,
    in-progress read 0, the agent chip read
    '● analyst', and the cap did NOT count it -
    so a second build could clobber the tree.
    Now: the phases write at ENTRY *and again*
    at EXIT (two writers on purpose), and -
    deterministically - the cap counts a live
    gogo-go session regardless of the
    file-derived class. A card in the residual
    launch-to-first-write window shows an amber
    '● building' cue and keeps its file-derived
    column (D6=A).
  end note

  note right of stalled
    Not a status on disk - a DERIVED cue: a
    working status (implementing / reviewing /
    testing) with no live session for the slug.
    The cost of an honest entry write is that a
    killed phase leaves 'implementing' behind,
    so it must read '· stalled' rather than
    running. Quiet glyph: it is resumable.
  end note

  state entry_write_skipped <<choice>>
  implementing --> entry_write_skipped: a phase BEGINS and does not record itself
  reviewing --> entry_write_skipped: (observed on ALL THREE review rounds of this feature)
  entry_write_skipped --> state_lags: events.jsonl contradicts the phase line
  state_lags --> reviewing: the user checks which half did not land
  state_lags --> implementing: (the cue names a disagreement, not a culprit)

  note right of state_lags
    The DETECTOR half, and the half that works:
    tui.phaseLineLags -> the '· state lags' cue.
    TWO arms, both needing a live BUILD session
    and a working status:
      A. newest event is phase-done for the very
         phase state.md names (a forward hand-off
         nothing claimed);
      B. newest event is an ENTRY event
         (phase-started / fix-round) naming a
         DIFFERENT phase than the line does (the
         loop BACK to implement, or a half-landed
         entry write).
    Arm B's shape is AMBIGUOUS by construction, so
    the honest reading is narrower than 'the phase
    line is stale': state.md and events.jsonl
    DISAGREE about the current phase - one half of
    step 1 did not land - and the user checks which.
    And SILENCE IS NOT PROOF OF HEALTH: a later
    mid-phase event overwrites the phase-done that
    arm A keys on, and the cue goes quiet while the
    phase line is still stale.
    With the exit write restored, arm A also blinks
    at every HEALTHY hand-off - accepted knowingly:
    a blink self-clears, a whole-phase glow does not.
  end note

  awaiting_uat --> shipped: /gogo:done (the acceptance)
  awaiting_uat --> waiting_for_user: UAT feedback locks the gate
  waiting_for_user --> plan_accepted: the user re-accepts the adjusted plan
  implementing --> waiting_for_user: a decision fork opens
  waiting_for_user --> implementing: /gogo:resume folds the answer back in

  note right of waiting_for_user
    A GATE, and since 0.29.0 the board enforces it:
    attemptActionForce refuses m AND M on a paused
    card (the guard used to exist for DISPLAY only,
    so the only thing between a keypress and a
    relaunch was a STOP instruction inside the
    spawned session's own prompt - prose guarding
    prose, this release's own counter-example).
    The refusal names the open decision BY ID and
    the right artifact: decisions.md + /gogo:resume
    for a plain fork, uat.md + re-acceptance for a
    UAT re-plan round (isUATReplan: a DIGIT after
    'uat', the same predicate the pill uses, so the
    bounce and the pill agree by construction).
    The exit write is SCOPED away from this status,
    so a card that enters the gate STAYS in it.
  end note
